# key_identity.ledger — the BARE ENTITY-NAME KEY census.
#
# Held by tests/logos/key_identity_lint.sh. Read that file's header for what a
# subject is, how the population is derived, and what each cell means; this file
# is the pin, not the argument.
#
# ROW: <file> <TAB> <registry> <TAB> <cell> <TAB> <count> <TAB> <normalised key expression>
#
# Line numbers are DELIBERATELY absent: they drift with every edit above them,
# and a ledger keyed on them goes red for reasons that have nothing to do with
# this class. The key expression is the site's identity and also documents it.
#
# CELLS — two DERIVED (the ledger can only agree with the tree, never assert
# onto it), two demanding a ground comment AT THE SITE:
#   O     qualified probe FIRST, bare slot LAST. Derived: a package-qualifying
#         probe appears within the preceding 900 characters. Delete that probe
#         and the cell flips — RED.
#   Q     the key IS a mangled link name, qualified by construction. Derived.
#   D     bare by a carried design decision; needs `KEY-IDENTITY:` at the site.
#   B#nn  bare and reachable, a KNOWN OPEN DEFECT; needs `KEY-IDENTITY: OPEN #nn`
#         at the site with the SAME task number.
#
# ⚠ A NEW ROW IS NOT A FORMALITY. It says a lookup in the compiler answers for
# whoever registered a name first. Qualify the key (sema_key / qualify_pkg, or
# a keymaker grown from them such as mlir_struct_key) before you add one.
#
# ⚠ HELD IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A REMOVED row is red too: a conversion is a
# deliberate ledger edit in the same commit, so the count can only move on
# purpose. A number that drifts down on its own is a measurement nobody reads.
#
# 41 rows / 44 sites. Cells: O 20, B#98 8, D 6, B#88 3, B#90 2, Q 2.

# ── FACT 3 pins: the scan-by-name guards (no map, same class) ───────────────
# `callee == "<bare name>"` comparisons in sema_expr.cpp, by region. The two
# guarded regions are pinned so a comparison moving between them is seen; the
# third is the one that matters most — a bare-name intrinsic comparison written
# outside BOTH guards is the wrong-answer instance regrowing.
#GUARD sema_expr_callee_eq_in_lower_call_guard 14
#GUARD sema_expr_callee_eq_in_lower_type_intrinsic 66
#GUARD sema_expr_callee_eq_unguarded 11

# ── FACT 4: THE SCAN-BY-NAME POPULATION, PER FILE, WITH A ROSTER DIGEST ─────
# FACT 3 pins the two guards in sema_expr.cpp STRUCTURALLY. That is necessary
# and it is NOT sufficient: this round's own verify planted a bare-name
# intercept in sema_stmt.cpp and the gate stayed green, and the tree already
# held `if (name == "AnyVal")` in mlir_gen.cpp — a SILENT WRONG ANSWER of
# exactly the shape FACT 3 exists to stop, in a file FACT 3 does not read.
#
# So the population is censused tree-wide instead: <file> <count> <digest of the
# sorted literals>. A new intercept moves the count in whatever file it is born
# in; a literal swapped for another at a constant count moves the digest. The
# gate does not classify the site — that is the human's job at the pin move —
# it only refuses to let one appear unnoticed.
#
# ROW: #SCAN <basename> <count> <md5-8 of the sorted literal list>
